      'English grammar requires a formal subject in every sentence ("It is getting dark", "It hurts to swallow", "There is not enough time"). Ukrainian syntax is much more flexible: one-member impersonal sentences express states of nature, luck, lack, and necessity directly without any pronoun subject!',
    rules: [
      '<b>1. Subjectless Nature & State Verbs (No Dummy "It"!)</b>: Where English uses dummy "it", Ukrainian uses a 3rd-person singular neuter verb or state word by itself:<ul><li><b>Nature transitions</b>: <b>Світа́є</b> (It is dawning), <b>Вечорі́є</b> (Evening is falling), <b>Сутені́є</b> (It is growing dark / dusk is falling), <b>Похолода́ло</b> (It has gotten colder).</li><li><b>Luck, Disposition & Sufficiency (+ Dative person + Genitive thing)</b>: <b>Мені́ пощасти́ло!</b> (I got lucky!), <b>Нам вистача́є ча́су</b> (We have enough time), <b>Мені́ браку́є слів</b> (I lack words / am at a loss for words), <b>Мені́ добре спи́ться</b> (I sleep well).</li></ul>',
      '<b>2. Predicative State Words & Short Adjectives (пе́вен, по́вен, ви́нен)</b>:<ul><li>Impersonal state words combine with a Dative experiencer + infinitive or clause: <b>Мені́ бо́ляче</b> (It hurts me), <b>Шкода́ / Жаль, що...</b> (It is a pity that...), <b>Вам слід відпочи́ти</b> (You ought to rest).</li><li>Four masculine adjectives have a crisp <b>short predicative form</b> when used after "am/is/was": <b>пе́вний → Я пе́вен</b> (I am sure; f. <b>Я пе́вна</b>), <b>по́вний → по́вен + Gen.</b> (full of: <i>за́л по́вен люде́й</i>), <b>ви́нний → ви́нен</b> (owing / guilty: <i>Скі́льки я вам ви́нен?</i>), and <b>ра́дий → рад / ра́дий</b> (glad).</li></ul>',
      '<b>3. Proportional Correlatives ("Чим..., тим..." = "The more..., the more...")</b>: To express proportional comparison ("The more you practice, the easier it gets"), pair <b>Чим + comparative</b> in the first clause with <b>тим + comparative</b> in the second clause: <b>Чим бі́льше я чита́ю украї́нською, тим кра́ще розумі́ю носі́їв мо́ви</b>. Also notice correlative pronouns linking complex clauses: <b>Той, хто...</b> (He who / The one who...), <b>Туди́, де...</b> (To the place where...).',
      '<b>4. Parenthetical Modal Words (Вставні́ слова́)</b>: Native speakers weave parenthetical words into conversation to show certainty, probability, or topic shifts (always set off by commas in writing): <b>ма́буть</b> (probably / most likely), <b>безумо́вно</b> (undoubtedly), <b>між і́ншим / до ре́чі</b> (by the way), <b>зре́штою</b> (after all / ultimately), <b>щопра́вда</b> (admittedly / to be sure).',
      '<b>5. Accusative vs. Dative Experiencers in Health Verbs & Sensory «Па́хне + Instrumental»</b>:<ul><li><b>Accusative Experiencer («мене́, його́, її́») for Physical Chills, Fever & Nausea</b>: While general state words take the Dative (<b>Мені́ хо́лодно, Мені́ не спи́ться, Мені́ здає́ться</b>), three high-frequency impersonal sickness verbs put the person in the <b>Accusative case</b>: <b>Мене́ ну́дить</b> (I feel nauseous), <b>Його́ лихома́нить</b> (He feels feverish), and <b>Мене́ моро́зить</b> (I feel chilled/shivery; past: <i>Тара́са нуди́ло</i>, future: <i>мене́ моро́зитиме</i>).</li><li><b>Impersonal Sensory «Па́хне + Instrumental» ("It Smells Of...")</b>: To say "There is a smell of X / It smells of X", Ukrainian uses impersonal <b>па́хне</b> (past: neuter <b>па́хло</b>) + the <b>Instrumental case</b>: <b>У са́ду па́хне кві́тами</b> (It smells of flowers in the garden); <b>У кав’я́рні па́хло сві́жою ка́вою</b> (It smelled of fresh coffee in the café).</li></ul>',
    ],
